---
// /for-manufacturers — manufacturer-track audience landing page. Full 8-step
// storyboard end-to-end, CEO-as-protagonist register, with the designer-
// demand callout in the Insight section to surface the dual-track activation
// flywheel without diluting the manufacturer pitch.
//
// Spec:          docs/tasks/sections/for-manufacturers.md
// Copy:          docs/growth/copy/for-manufacturers.md
// Positioning:   docs/growth/positioning-manufacturer.md
// Storyboard:    docs/growth/sales-pitch-storyboard-manufacturer.md

import PageShell from '../components/PageShell.astro';
import StickyToc from '../components/StickyToc.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import CrossLinkFooter from '../components/CrossLinkFooter.astro';
import DiscoveryLoopDiagram from '../components/DiscoveryLoopDiagram.astro';
import ManufacturerFlowDiagram from '../components/ManufacturerFlowDiagram.astro';
import StandardsTimelineDiagram from '../components/StandardsTimelineDiagram.astro';

const tocItems = [
  { href: '#insight', label: 'Where discovery is going' },
  { href: '#alternatives', label: 'The four approaches' },
  { href: '#perfect-world', label: 'What winning looks like' },
  { href: '#introduction', label: 'What ULC is' },
  { href: '#themes', label: 'Differentiated value' },
];

const crossLinks = [
  { href: '/validator', labelHtml: 'Try the validator on a candidate <code>.ulc</code> file' },
  { href: '/docs', labelHtml: 'Authoring patterns and PIM mapping guides' },
  { href: '/docs#how-to-use-today', labelHtml: 'Two-tier consumption deep dive' },
  { href: '/governance', labelHtml: 'Stewardship and open license' },
  { href: '/#adopters', labelHtml: 'See current adopters' },
  { href: '/for-designers', labelHtml: 'Demand side: the designer track' },
];

const pageTitle = 'ULC for luminaire manufacturers: the AI-discovery layer';
const pageDescription = 'Specifier discovery is shifting from PDF to AI-mediated. The manufacturers whose data is machine-readable are the ones AI surfaces. Pilot a publication.';
---

<PageShell title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  {/* Hero band */}
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="dot-grid" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div
      class="relative mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:px-10 md:pt-24 md:pb-16 lg:pt-28 lg:pb-20"
    >
      <p class="small-caps text-xs md:text-sm text-[var(--text-muted)] mb-6">
        Manufacturer track
      </p>
      <h1
        class="hero-display max-w-5xl text-balance break-words font-display font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text)] text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-[3.25rem] leading-[1.1]"
      >
        For lighting manufacturers
      </h1>
      <p
        class="mt-6 md:mt-8 max-w-3xl text-base md:text-lg lg:text-xl text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed"
      >
        Specifier discovery is shifting from PDF to AI-mediated. The manufacturers whose data is structured and machine-readable are the ones AI tools surface, the ones design tools consume natively, and the ones designers ask for by name.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Body: 2-col on desktop with sticky TOC sidebar, single-col on mobile */}
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 md:px-10 pb-24 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[16rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-12">
    <div class="lg:pt-12">
      <StickyToc items={tocItems} />
    </div>

    <article class="long-form pt-10 lg:pt-12 max-w-[68ch]">

      {/* ── 1. INSIGHT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <section id="insight" aria-labelledby="insight-heading">
        <p class="step-num">Step 1</p>
        <h2 id="insight-heading">Where specifier discovery is going</h2>
        <p>
          Specifier discovery is moving from the designer opening a PDF to the designer asking an AI which luminaire fits a project. The manufacturers whose data is machine-readable are the ones that surface. Eventually, the ones that get the usage telemetry back. The bottleneck for that whole loop is not the AI. It is the data layer underneath, which today is PDF.
        </p>
        <DiscoveryLoopDiagram />

        <Callout intent="accent" label="Designer demand, in motion">
          <p>
            Designers are starting to ask their preferred manufacturer reps for ULC files. The activation flywheel turns through designer-to-rep advocacy. The demand-side surface is at <a href="/for-designers" class="link-inline">/for-designers</a>.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <p>
          The bottleneck is solvable today with structured publishing, ahead of the analytics loop that does not yet exist. The first manufacturers to publish are the ones the industry remembers as having defined the layer.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* ── 2. ALTERNATIVES ────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <section id="alternatives" aria-labelledby="alternatives-heading">
        <p class="step-num">Step 2</p>
        <h2 id="alternatives-heading">The four approaches</h2>
        <p>
          If you have decided to take the AI-mediated discovery shift seriously, there are four approaches. Three are status quo or variants; the fourth is ULC.
        </p>

        <table class="alt-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Approach</th>
              <th scope="col">Pros</th>
              <th scope="col">Cons</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Status quo: PDF + IES + LDT alone</th>
              <td>Zero new investment. The publishing workflow you already run.</td>
              <td>Extraction is wrong on attributes that matter (CCT, CRI, optical accessories, IP rating). The aggregator's interpretation, not your authoritative file, becomes what the designer's tool reads.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Outsource to aggregators and PIM platforms</th>
              <td>Someone else does the structured publishing. Predictable cost.</td>
              <td>The aggregator becomes the data layer between you and the designer's tool. You are paying to be re-interpreted. Hashes, provenance, and corrections all live downstream of your file.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Proprietary in-house JSON or API</th>
              <td>Maximum control over format and timing. No standards-committee work.</td>
              <td>Every AI consumer needs a per-OEM parser. Does not scale: the consumer-side cost is multiplied by the number of manufacturers. No interoperability dividend.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">GLDF (XML, DIAL ecosystem)</th>
              <td>Mature in continental Europe. Embedded in DIALux planning workflows.</td>
              <td>XML. Planning-centric, not AI-consumption-centric. Steward is a software-vendor body with commercial planning tools. JSON support and AI-consumption framing are not on the roadmap today.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="alt-row--companion">
              <th scope="row">The fifth row: ULC</th>
              <td colspan="2">
                Open, JSON, AI-consumption-first. Metadata-only with hash-referenced source files. Neutrally stewarded. See the themes section below for what it delivers and The Pilot for what it costs.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      {/* ── 3. PERFECT WORLD ───────────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <section id="perfect-world" aria-labelledby="perfect-heading">
        <p class="step-num">Step 3</p>
        <h2 id="perfect-heading">What "winning the AI-mediated discovery layer" looks like</h2>
        <p>
          A CEO who wants their brand to win in the AI-mediated discovery layer is committing to three criteria.
        </p>

        <ol class="criteria-list">
          <li>
            <h3>Your products surface in the answer set with accurate attributes.</h3>
            <p>
              When a designer asks an AI which 2700K downlight under 40W fits a project, you are in the answer with the right CCT, the right CRI, the right optical accessories, the right IP rating. Not whatever the extraction layer guessed off your PDF.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>The data the designer's tool consumes comes from you, verifiable to your source files.</h3>
            <p>
              No aggregator's interpretation, no spec database's re-extraction sitting in the middle. You own the data relationship at the data layer. Your existing rep, distributor, and aggregator relationships stay where they are.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>The format you publish in is an open industry standard you helped shape.</h3>
            <p>
              Not a vendor's proprietary platform. Not an aggregator's schema. Neutrally stewarded, with a clear path to becoming the table-stakes layer the whole industry adopts, and a seat at the table while it is being shaped.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <Callout intent="accent">
          <p>
            If these three feel right, ULC is the layer you have been waiting for. If you reject one of them, ULC is not the right standard for your brand. That is a real answer, not a failure.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      {/* ── 4. INTRODUCTION ────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <section id="introduction" aria-labelledby="introduction-heading">
        <p class="step-num">Step 4</p>
        <h2 id="introduction-heading">What ULC is</h2>
        <p>
          ULC is the open standard for luminaire product data. The machine-readable layer AI tools, design tools, and spec databases read alongside your existing PDF, IES, and LDT.
        </p>
        <ManufacturerFlowDiagram />
        <p>
          Today, you publish PDF + IES + LDT separately. An extraction layer (AI tool or aggregator pipeline) tries to read structure from your PDF, lossy. An aggregator or spec database re-publishes its interpretation. The designer's tool consumes the aggregator's interpretation, with errors.
        </p>
        <p>
          With ULC, you publish PDF + IES + LDT + a ULC record. Source files are referenced by SHA-256 hash, not embedded. The designer's AI or design tool fetches your ULC record directly, verifies the hash, parses native JSON.
        </p>
        <Callout intent="default" label="What ULC replaces vs integrates with">
          <p>
            ULC does not replace your PDF, your IES, your LDT, your rep agency, your distributor, your existing PIM, or any existing commercial relationship. It integrates with your existing PIM via the published mapping guides (Salsify, Akeneo, SAP, custom).
          </p>
          <p>
            What it replaces is the aggregator's interpretation as the data layer the designer's tool actually reads. The aggregator can still exist; it is no longer the authoritative source.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      {/* ── 5. DIFFERENTIATED VALUE (3 themes) ─────────────────────────── */}
      <section id="themes" aria-labelledby="themes-heading">
        <p class="step-num">Step 5</p>
        <h2 id="themes-heading">Differentiated value, three themes</h2>

        {/* Theme 1 */}
        <section id="theme-1" class="theme" aria-labelledby="theme-1-heading">
          <p class="theme__num">Theme 1</p>
          <h3 id="theme-1-heading">Found in AI-mediated specifier discovery.</h3>
          <p>
            ULC delivers inclusion in AI-mediated specifier workflows for specification-grade luminaire manufacturers. When a designer asks an AI which 2700K downlight under 40W with a specific optic and CRI fits a project, you are in the answer set with accurate attributes. Not whatever the extraction layer guessed off your PDF.
          </p>

          <Callout intent="default" label="One canonical record">
            <p>
              One of the four canonical reference records in <code>ulcspec/ULC/examples/</code> is Pattern B, the Selux Aya pole. A single product cutsheet covering 54,000 SKUs across configurator axes and CCT multipliers compressed into 21 ULC records via the <code>applicability</code> block.
            </p>
            <p>
              The PDF stays beautiful for the human reader. The 21 ULC records make every configurator axis machine-visible.
            </p>
          </Callout>

          <div class="contrast">
            <div class="contrast__col">
              <p class="contrast__label">From PDF extraction today</p>
              <ul class="contrast__list">
                <li>Paraphrased CCT</li>
                <li>Missing optical accessories</li>
                <li>Occasionally wrong CRI</li>
                <li>Mounting unclear</li>
                <li>IP rating depending on which footnote was read</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="contrast__col contrast__col--gain">
              <p class="contrast__label">From a ULC record</p>
              <ul class="contrast__list">
                <li>Typed fields</li>
                <li>Taxonomy-conformant values</li>
                <li>Explicit-missing-fields surfacing</li>
                <li>Source-file hash verification</li>
                <li>Same product, machine-visible</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="contrast-note">
            Same product. Two completely different consumption paths.
          </p>

          <Callout intent="accent" label="Upward pitch">
            <p>
              "When a designer asks an AI which 2700K downlight under 40W to spec, we are in the answer. Our competitors who publish only PDFs are not."
            </p>
          </Callout>

          <p class="return-line">
            That is how ULC gets you found in AI-mediated specifier discovery, with your attributes correct, sourced from you, verifiable.
          </p>
        </section>

        {/* Theme 2 */}
        <section id="theme-2" class="theme" aria-labelledby="theme-2-heading">
          <p class="theme__num">Theme 2</p>
          <h3 id="theme-2-heading">Direct specifier-to-manufacturer data channel.</h3>
          <p>
            ULC delivers a direct data channel between you and the designer's tool. The AI agent, the design tool, the spec database fetches your authoritative ULC file from your infrastructure, verifies it against your source files by SHA-256 hash, and parses it natively. The aggregator's interpretation is no longer the data layer the designer sees.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your rep relationships, your distributor relationships, your aggregator listings, all stay where they are. The change is at the data layer, not the commercial layer.
          </p>

          <Callout intent="default" label="Hash verification, briefly">
            <p>
              The <code>sources[]</code> block in a ULC record references your PDF, your IES, and your LDT by URL and SHA-256 hash. Any consumer can re-hash the source file and prove they are reading the exact artifact you published. No embedding. No redistribution. Just verification.
            </p>
          </Callout>

          <pre class="code-block" aria-label="Example sources block from a ULC record"><code>{`{
  "sources": [
    {
      "type": "datasheet_pdf",
      "url": "https://example-manufacturer.com/products/spec-sheet.pdf",
      "sha256": "a3f5b8…"
    },
    {
      "type": "ies",
      "url": "https://example-manufacturer.com/products/photometric.ies",
      "sha256": "c8e2a1…"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

          <Callout intent="default" label="IP safety, at the architecture layer">
            <p>
              The ULC record is metadata only. It references your source files by hash and URL. It does not embed them. Your PDF stays your PDF. Your IES stays your IES. Your LDT stays your LDT. The IP framing is settled at the architecture layer, not negotiated per pilot.
            </p>
          </Callout>

          <Callout intent="accent" label="Upward pitch">
            <p>
              "For the first time, the designer's tools fetch our spec data directly from us. Not a rep agency's interpretation, not a spec database's re-extraction."
            </p>
          </Callout>

          <p class="return-line">
            That is how ULC gives you a direct data channel to the designer's tools without disturbing the commercial relationships you already have.
          </p>
        </section>

        {/* Theme 3 */}
        <section id="theme-3" class="theme" aria-labelledby="theme-3-heading">
          <p class="theme__num">Theme 3</p>
          <h3 id="theme-3-heading">Lead the industry's transition to machine-readable product data.</h3>
          <p>
            ULC delivers early-mover positioning for the manufacturers who publish first and help shape the standard. ULC is on an open-standard trajectory: MIT-licensed, neutrally stewarded by a practicing designer with MIES, IALD, and CLD credentials, with active dialogue with DIAL (DIALux, GLDF), LIA, and IES, planned crosswalks to GLDF and ETIM, and pilot conversations underway.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first manufacturers to publish are the ones the industry will remember as having defined the layer. Later adopters get the standard once it is table-stakes; early adopters get to shape it.
          </p>

          <StandardsTimelineDiagram />

          <Callout intent="default" label="Governance and license">
            <p>
              MIT license. <code>GOVERNANCE.md</code> documents who maintains, how decisions flow, how stewardship transitions. The Schema Change Proposal process means schema evolution does not depend on one party. The steward is a lighting professional, not a software-vendor body with a commercial roadmap.
            </p>
          </Callout>

          <p class="dialogue-strip" aria-label="ULC is in active dialogue with DIAL, LIA, and IES">
            In active dialogue with <strong>DIAL</strong> <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> <strong>LIA</strong> <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> <strong>IES</strong>
          </p>

          <Callout intent="accent" label="Upward pitch">
            <p>
              "We helped define the standard our peers will all adopt by 2030. We are not adopting it, we are shaping it."
            </p>
          </Callout>

          <p class="return-line">
            That is how ULC lets you lead the industry's transition to machine-readable product data, instead of catching up to it.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

    </article>
  </div>

  <CrossLinkFooter links={crossLinks} />
</PageShell>

<style>
  /* ── Long-form prose, manufacturer-document register ─────────────────
     Matches /for-designers' .long-form so the two audience surfaces share
     a single prose token system. Any change here must land on /for-designers
     too; the next long-form surface (/docs) consolidates these as
     prose-* tokens in DESIGN_SYSTEM.md (deferred to PR-A2 per
     for-designers-plan §10.4). */
  .long-form {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .long-form > section {
    margin-top: 4.5rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .long-form > section:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .long-form p {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem 0;
    max-width: 68ch;
  }
  .long-form p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .long-form strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .long-form code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.95em;
    color: var(--accent-warm);
  }
  .long-form a {
    color: var(--accent-warm);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .long-form a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-warm-deep);
  }

  .step-num {
    font-variant-caps: all-small-caps;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-subtle);
    margin: 0 0 0.625rem 0 !important;
  }

  .long-form h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 1.875rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem 0;
    max-width: 28ch;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .long-form h2 {
      font-size: 2.25rem;
    }
  }
  .long-form h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0.75rem 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .long-form h3 {
      font-size: 1.375rem;
    }
  }

  /* ── Alternatives table ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .alt-table {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    display: block;
  }
  .alt-table thead,
  .alt-table tbody,
  .alt-table tr {
    display: block;
  }
  .alt-table thead {
    display: none;
  }
  .alt-table tr {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1rem 1.125rem;
  }
  .alt-table tr:last-child {
    border-bottom: none;
  }
  .alt-table th[scope='row'],
  .alt-table td {
    display: block;
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .alt-table th[scope='row'] {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }
  .alt-table td + td {
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .alt-table {
      display: table;
    }
    .alt-table thead {
      display: table-header-group;
      background: var(--bg);
    }
    .alt-table tbody {
      display: table-row-group;
    }
    .alt-table tr {
      display: table-row;
      padding: 0;
    }
    .alt-table th,
    .alt-table td {
      display: table-cell;
      padding: 1.125rem 1.25rem;
      vertical-align: top;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .alt-table tr:last-child th,
    .alt-table tr:last-child td {
      border-bottom: none;
    }
    .alt-table th[scope='col'] {
      font-variant-caps: all-small-caps;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      color: var(--text-subtle);
      text-align: left;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .alt-table th[scope='row'] {
      margin-bottom: 0;
      width: 30%;
    }
    .alt-table td + td {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  }
  /* Companion row (the ULC fifth row) — matches sibling /for-designers
     neutral 4% mix so the two surfaces share one alt-table treatment.
     The accent-warm row header keeps the ULC name highlighted without
     repainting the whole row. */
  .alt-table .alt-row--companion {
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--text-subtle) 4%, var(--bg-elevated));
  }
  .alt-table .alt-row--companion th[scope='row'] {
    color: var(--accent-warm);
  }

  /* ── Criteria list ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .criteria-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    counter-reset: criterion;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .criteria-list > li {
    counter-increment: criterion;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-warm);
    padding: 0.125rem 0 0.125rem 1.25rem;
    position: relative;
  }
  .criteria-list > li::before {
    content: counter(criterion, decimal-leading-zero);
    position: absolute;
    left: -2.5rem;
    top: 0.25rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-subtle);
    display: none;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .criteria-list > li {
      padding-left: 1.5rem;
    }
    .criteria-list > li::before {
      display: block;
    }
    .criteria-list {
      padding-left: 2.5rem;
    }
  }
  .criteria-list h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .criteria-list h3 {
      font-size: 1.125rem;
    }
  }
  .criteria-list p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin: 0.375rem 0 0 0;
  }

  /* ── Theme blocks (3) ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .theme {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem !important;
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .theme:first-of-type {
    border-top: none;
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  .theme__num {
    font-variant-caps: all-small-caps;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--accent-warm);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0 !important;
  }
  .theme h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    max-width: 32ch;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .theme h3 {
      font-size: 1.625rem;
    }
  }
  .theme .return-line {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-warm);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  /* ── Theme 1 extraction-vs-ULC contrast block ────────────────────── */
  .contrast {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .contrast {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 1rem;
    }
  }
  .contrast__col {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
    padding: 1rem 1.125rem;
  }
  .contrast__col--gain {
    border-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent-warm) 35%, var(--border));
